# D119 Batch 1: ADHD (15) + Asthma Pediatric (12) + Autism (10) = 37 questions
questions = [
  # ===== ADHD (15) =====
  {"topic":"ADHD","course":"D119","q":"A 7-year-old boy is referred for evaluation after his teacher reports he cannot stay in his seat, frequently interrupts classmates, and loses his homework daily. His parents report similar behavior at home. How many settings must symptoms be present to diagnose ADHD per DSM-5?","a":["One setting only (school)","Two or more settings","Three or more settings","Any setting if severe enough"],"correct":1,"rationale":"DSM-5 requires ADHD symptoms to be present in two or more settings (e.g., home AND school) to confirm the diagnosis and rule out situational causes. The core symptom clusters are inattention (≥6 symptoms) and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity (≥6 symptoms) persisting ≥6 months. **Board tip:** ADHD must cause functional impairment, begin before age 12, and be present in multiple settings — not just one environment."},
  {"topic":"ADHD","course":"D119","q":"A 9-year-old with newly diagnosed ADHD-combined type has no cardiac history. Before starting stimulant therapy, which screening step is MOST important?","a":["Fasting lipid panel","Cardiac history and family history of sudden cardiac death","EEG baseline","Thyroid function tests"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Before initiating stimulant medications, a thorough personal and family cardiac history should be obtained (history of structural heart disease, arrhythmia, sudden unexplained death in young relatives). Routine EKG is NOT recommended unless history suggests cardiac risk. **Board tip:** AAP does not recommend routine EKG before stimulants, but a cardiac history is mandatory — if concerning, refer to cardiology first."},
  {"topic":"ADHD","course":"D119","q":"Which stimulant medication is FDA-approved as first-line treatment for ADHD in children age 6 and older?","a":["Atomoxetine (Strattera)","Methylphenidate (Ritalin) or amphetamine salts (Adderall)","Guanfacine (Intuniv)","Bupropion (Wellbutrin)"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Stimulant medications — methylphenidate and amphetamine-based agents — are first-line FDA-approved pharmacotherapy for ADHD in children ≥6 years. They have the largest evidence base and highest efficacy. **Board tip:** For children 4-5 years old, behavior therapy ALONE is recommended before medications. For school-age children, combined therapy (medication + behavioral) is optimal."},
  {"topic":"ADHD","course":"D119","q":"A 10-year-old on methylphenidate 18mg daily reports decreased appetite and difficulty sleeping. What is the best management strategy?","a":["Switch immediately to atomoxetine","Reduce dose and give medication earlier in the day; protein-rich breakfast before dose","Discontinue stimulant and start guanfacine","Add melatonin and continue same regimen without change"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Appetite suppression and insomnia are the most common stimulant side effects. Management: give medication earlier (avoid late afternoon doses for sleep), encourage high-calorie breakfast before the dose, and monitor growth. Medication holidays on weekends can help appetite during growth spurts. **Board tip:** Monitor height/weight every 6 months on stimulants — significant growth deceleration may necessitate dose reduction or drug holiday."},
  {"topic":"ADHD","course":"D119","q":"A teenage girl with ADHD is doing poorly academically despite adequate methylphenidate dosing. Her mother is resistant to increasing the dose. Which non-stimulant medication is FDA-approved for ADHD and may also help with comorbid anxiety?","a":["Clonidine (Kapvay)","Atomoxetine (Strattera)","Bupropion","Modafinil"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Atomoxetine (Strattera) is a selective norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor — the first non-stimulant FDA-approved for ADHD in children, adolescents, and adults. It takes 4-8 weeks for full effect but has benefit in patients with comorbid anxiety and is not a controlled substance. **Board tip:** Atomoxetine carries a black box warning for suicidal ideation in children/adolescents — monitor closely in the first few months."},
  {"topic":"ADHD","course":"D119","q":"For a child with ADHD and comorbid Tourette syndrome, which medication class should be used with CAUTION due to risk of worsening tics?","a":["Alpha-2 agonists (guanfacine, clonidine)","Atomoxetine","Stimulants (methylphenidate, amphetamines)","Antidepressants (bupropion)"],"correct":2,"rationale":"Stimulants can worsen or unmask tics in children with Tourette syndrome, though evidence is mixed. Alpha-2 agonists (guanfacine, clonidine) are preferred in ADHD + tic disorder because they treat both ADHD and tics. **Board tip:** If tics worsen on stimulants, consider switching to guanfacine extended-release (Intuniv), which is FDA-approved for ADHD and can reduce tic frequency."},
  {"topic":"ADHD","course":"D119","q":"A parent asks about behavioral therapy for their 5-year-old with ADHD. Per AAP guidelines, what is the recommended FIRST-LINE treatment for preschool-age children (4-5 years)?","a":["Methylphenidate immediately","Parent training in behavior management (PTBM) before any medication","Amphetamine salts — FDA approved at age 3","Atomoxetine plus behavioral therapy simultaneously"],"correct":1,"rationale":"AAP guidelines strongly recommend parent training in behavior management (PTBM) as FIRST-LINE treatment for preschool children (4-5 years) with ADHD before considering medications. The preschool brain is highly neuroplastic, and behavioral interventions have strong evidence. **Board tip:** For ages 4-5, prescribe behavior therapy first. Medications are added only if behavior therapy fails and symptoms remain moderate-to-severe."},
  {"topic":"ADHD","course":"D119","q":"Monitoring parameters for a child on long-term stimulant therapy should include which of the following at EVERY visit?","a":["Complete blood count and liver function tests","Blood pressure, heart rate, height, weight, and ADHD symptom rating scales","Renal function panel and electrolytes","Brain MRI every 2 years"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Stimulant monitoring: BP and HR (stimulants increase both), height and weight (growth monitoring), and standardized rating scales (e.g., Vanderbilt, Conners) to assess treatment efficacy at every visit. **Board tip:** If BP or HR is elevated, consider dose reduction or switch to non-stimulant. Growth deceleration >2 standard deviations warrants reassessment."},
  {"topic":"ADHD","course":"D119","q":"A 16-year-old with well-controlled ADHD on mixed amphetamine salts (Adderall XR) wants to start driving. What key counseling should the FNP provide?","a":["Driving is contraindicated while on stimulants","He should take his medication before driving — stimulants improve reaction time and reduce crash risk in ADHD","Restrict driving to daylight hours only","Stop medications before operating any vehicle"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Teens with untreated ADHD have significantly higher crash rates than peers. Stimulant medication taken before driving REDUCES crash risk in ADHD patients. Research shows untreated ADHD impairs driving performance substantially. **Board tip:** Counsel teens that medication adherence is especially important when driving. Untreated ADHD = 3x higher crash risk. Medication compliance should be emphasized."},
  {"topic":"ADHD","course":"D119","q":"A parent reports her 8-year-old seems sad and cries easily since starting stimulant medication 3 weeks ago. What is the most likely explanation?","a":["The child has developed bipolar disorder","Stimulant rebound effect or emotional lability as a side effect","The medication dose is too low","This is a sign of serotonin syndrome"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Emotional lability, crying, irritability, and mood changes (often called the 'rebound effect') are recognized side effects of stimulant medications, particularly as the medication wears off in the afternoon. **Board tip:** If emotional symptoms persist throughout the day, consider dose adjustment, switching formulations (short-acting to long-acting), or evaluating for comorbid depression — which co-occurs in ~20% of ADHD children."},
  {"topic":"ADHD","course":"D119","q":"Which DSM-5 ADHD presentation is characterized by difficulty sustaining attention, losing things, being forgetful, and daydreaming — WITHOUT hyperactivity or impulsivity?","a":["ADHD, Combined Presentation","ADHD, Predominantly Hyperactive-Impulsive Presentation","ADHD, Predominantly Inattentive Presentation (formerly ADD)","ADHD, Unspecified Presentation"],"correct":2,"rationale":"Predominantly Inattentive ADHD (formerly called ADD) presents with ≥6 inattention symptoms but fewer than 6 hyperactivity-impulsivity symptoms. These children are often 'quiet underachievers' who are missed because they don't disrupt the classroom. **Board tip:** Girls are more often diagnosed with inattentive type — they tend to daydream rather than act out, leading to underdiagnosis and later identification."},
  {"topic":"ADHD","course":"D119","q":"An 11-year-old with ADHD has not responded adequately to methylphenidate (tried 2 formulations at adequate doses). What is the appropriate next step?","a":["Reassess the ADHD diagnosis and try amphetamine-based stimulant","Refer to neurology for brain MRI","Add an antipsychotic medication","Discontinue all medications and use behavioral therapy only"],"correct":0,"rationale":"If one stimulant class (methylphenidate-based) fails, try the other class (amphetamine-based, e.g., Adderall, Vyvanse) before considering non-stimulants. Response rates differ between classes. 70-80% of children respond to one stimulant class. **Board tip:** Always reassess the diagnosis if medications aren't working — confirm the diagnosis is correct, evaluate for comorbidities (anxiety, learning disability, sleep disorder), and ensure adequate dosing."},
  {"topic":"ADHD","course":"D119","q":"Guanfacine extended-release (Intuniv) is approved for ADHD. What class of medication is this and what is its main mechanism?","a":["Stimulant — dopamine reuptake inhibitor","Alpha-2 adrenergic agonist — reduces norepinephrine release in prefrontal cortex","SNRI — serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor","Antidepressant — dopamine enhancer"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Guanfacine ER (Intuniv) is an alpha-2A adrenergic agonist that strengthens prefrontal cortical connections by modulating norepinephrine signaling, improving working memory, attention, and impulse control. It is FDA-approved for ADHD ages 6-17 as monotherapy or adjunct. **Board tip:** Common side effects include somnolence, fatigue, and hypotension. Do NOT abruptly discontinue — taper to avoid rebound hypertension."},
  {"topic":"ADHD","course":"D119","q":"Which tool is the gold standard for monitoring ADHD treatment response in the school-age child?","a":["In-office cognitive testing (IQ test)","Vanderbilt Assessment Scale completed by parents AND teachers","Child Behavior Checklist (CBCL) parent report alone","Clinician observation during one office visit"],"correct":1,"rationale":"The Vanderbilt ADHD Diagnostic Rating Scale is the AAP-endorsed tool for ADHD diagnosis and treatment monitoring. It must be completed by BOTH parents AND teachers to capture cross-setting symptoms (DSM-5 requirement). **Board tip:** Treatment monitoring requires follow-up Vanderbilt scales from both home and school — not just parent report. Schedule 1-month follow-up after medication initiation, then every 6 months when stable."},
  {"topic":"ADHD","course":"D119","q":"A 14-year-old patient requests a stimulant prescription for ADHD. The FNP suspects diversion (selling medication to peers). What is the most appropriate clinical response?","a":["Refuse to treat ADHD in teenagers","Prescribe non-stimulant alternatives as first-line due to diversion risk; counsel about misuse","Prescribe only if parents are present in the room","Call law enforcement immediately"],"correct":1,"rationale":"In adolescents with suspected diversion risk, non-stimulant alternatives (atomoxetine, guanfacine) or long-acting formulations that are harder to crush/snort (Vyvanse/lisdexamfetamine — prodrug) are preferred. Thorough counseling about medication misuse, legal consequences of sharing medications, and close monitoring are essential. **Board tip:** Lisdexamfetamine (Vyvanse) is a prodrug requiring GI conversion to be active, making it less abusable than immediate-release formulations."},

  # ===== ASTHMA PEDIATRIC (12) =====
  {"topic":"Asthma Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 6-year-old with persistent asthma uses albuterol for symptoms more than 2 days/week and wakes at night with coughing twice a month. What is her asthma severity classification?","a":["Intermittent","Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Severe persistent"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Mild persistent asthma: symptoms >2 days/week but not daily, nighttime awakenings 3-4x/month, SABA use >2 days/week, no limitation in normal activity. Step 2 therapy is indicated (low-dose ICS). **Board tip:** Classify severity BEFORE treatment is initiated; once on controller therapy, assess CONTROL (well-controlled, not well-controlled, very poorly controlled)."},
  {"topic":"Asthma Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"Which controller medication is first-line for mild persistent asthma in a 5-year-old child?","a":["Long-acting beta-agonist (LABA) alone","Low-dose inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) daily","Montelukast (Singulair) alone","Oral prednisone daily"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Low-dose inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) are the preferred first-line controller therapy (Step 2) for mild persistent asthma in all age groups. They reduce airway inflammation and prevent exacerbations more effectively than any alternative. **Board tip:** Montelukast is an alternative but is LESS effective than ICS. LABAs should NEVER be used as monotherapy in asthma — always combine with ICS."},
  {"topic":"Asthma Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 4-year-old with asthma is prescribed an albuterol MDI. What device should ALWAYS be prescribed with an MDI for a child this age?","a":["Peak flow meter","Valved holding chamber (spacer)","Nebulizer mask only — MDIs are contraindicated under age 5","Dry powder inhaler"],"correct":1,"rationale":"A valved holding chamber (spacer/VHC) is MANDATORY with MDI for children <5 years and should be used in all young children. It eliminates the need for perfect hand-breath coordination, increases drug deposition in the lungs, and reduces oral candidiasis from ICS. **Board tip:** Always prescribe a spacer with any MDI for children. Instruct parents on proper cleaning (soap and water weekly) and spacer replacement every 6-12 months."},
  {"topic":"Asthma Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"During an acute asthma exacerbation in a 7-year-old, which medication is the FIRST to administer?","a":["Systemic corticosteroids (oral prednisone)","Short-acting beta-agonist (SABA) — albuterol via nebulizer or MDI+spacer","Ipratropium bromide (Atrovent)","Magnesium sulfate IV"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Short-acting beta-agonist (SABA/albuterol) is the FIRST and primary rescue medication for acute asthma — it provides rapid bronchodilation within minutes. Systemic corticosteroids are added for moderate-to-severe exacerbations to reduce inflammation. **Board tip:** For severe exacerbations unresponsive to initial SABA, add ipratropium (anticholinergic) and give systemic corticosteroids early. Magnesium sulfate is reserved for life-threatening exacerbations."},
  {"topic":"Asthma Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A mother asks why her child's doctor prescribed budesonide inhaler even when her daughter 'feels fine.' What is the best explanation?","a":["The doctor made an error — ICS should only be used during attacks","Inhaled corticosteroids are controller medications that reduce underlying inflammation daily, preventing future attacks","ICS are addictive and the child needs higher doses over time","The ICS will help her daughter grow taller"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Inhaled corticosteroids are controller (maintenance) medications that work by reducing chronic airway inflammation daily — they do NOT provide immediate symptom relief. Parents must understand that ICS must be used every day even when asymptomatic to prevent exacerbations. **Board tip:** Adherence education is critical. Common parental concern: 'steroid fear.' Reassure that ICS doses used in asthma are very low and local (not systemic) — growth effects are minimal and benefits outweigh risks."},
  {"topic":"Asthma Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 10-year-old with moderate persistent asthma is well-controlled on low-dose ICS + LABA. His mother wants to stop the LABA. What is the correct counseling?","a":["LABAs can be safely stopped any time in children","LABA monotherapy is acceptable as step-down once well-controlled","LABAs should NEVER be used without ICS in children due to increased asthma mortality risk","LABA can be replaced by montelukast at any time"],"correct":2,"rationale":"LABAs (salmeterol, formoterol) carry an FDA black box warning: NEVER use without concurrent ICS in pediatric patients due to increased risk of asthma-related death. Fixed-dose ICS/LABA combination inhalers (Advair, Symbicort) reduce this risk by ensuring ICS adherence. **Board tip:** The LABA black box warning is a high-yield board topic. LABAs alone in asthma = contraindicated in children."},
  {"topic":"Asthma Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A child's peak flow meter reading is 60% of personal best. According to the asthma action plan 'traffic light' system, what zone does this represent and what action is needed?","a":["Green zone — continue regular medications","Yellow zone — use rescue inhaler and contact provider","Red zone — emergency, go to ER","Blue zone — increase ICS temporarily"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Peak flow 50-79% of personal best = Yellow Zone (Caution). Action: use rescue SABA promptly, follow asthma action plan, contact provider if no improvement. <50% = Red Zone (emergency/ER). ≥80% = Green Zone (doing well). **Board tip:** Every asthmatic child should have a written asthma action plan based on peak flow zones or symptom criteria. The peak flow meter is most useful in school-age children ≥5 years."},
  {"topic":"Asthma Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"Which asthma trigger is the MOST common precipitant of acute exacerbations in school-age children?","a":["Cold air","Viral upper respiratory infections","Pet dander","Exercise"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Viral upper respiratory infections (especially rhinovirus) are the #1 trigger for asthma exacerbations in school-age children, particularly during fall cold/flu season. This explains why asthma hospitalizations peak in September ('September epidemic'). **Board tip:** Also counsel on other major triggers: environmental allergens, smoke exposure, exercise, cold air, and strong odors. Annual influenza vaccine is recommended for all asthmatic children."},
  {"topic":"Asthma Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 3-year-old has frequent wheezing with viral infections but is asymptomatic between illnesses and has no allergies, no family history of asthma, and normal exam. What is the most likely diagnosis?","a":["Persistent asthma requiring daily ICS","Viral-induced wheeze/episodic viral wheeze — not yet diagnostic of asthma","Cystic fibrosis","Laryngomalacia"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Episodic viral wheeze is common in toddlers (<3 years) who wheeze ONLY with viral infections and are symptom-free between episodes. This pattern does NOT necessarily represent asthma. The Asthma Predictive Index (API) helps stratify risk. **Board tip:** API risk factors for developing asthma: parental asthma, atopic dermatitis, allergen sensitization, peripheral eosinophilia. High-risk children benefit more from early ICS controller therapy."},
  {"topic":"Asthma Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"Exercise-induced bronchoconstriction (EIB) in a 12-year-old athlete — what is the first-line treatment before exercise?","a":["Daily montelukast","Pre-exercise albuterol MDI (2 puffs) 15-30 minutes before activity","Daily ICS + LABA combination","Avoid all exercise"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Pre-exercise albuterol (SABA) 2 puffs 15-30 minutes before activity is first-line for EIB. It provides 2-4 hours of bronchoprotection. Daily controller therapy (ICS) should be considered if EIB is frequent or associated with persistent asthma. **Board tip:** EIB affects 40% of competitive athletes. Children with EIB should NOT avoid exercise — proper pre-treatment allows full athletic participation. Montelukast is an alternative for EIB in those who don't respond to pre-exercise albuterol."},
  {"topic":"Asthma Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 9-year-old with asthma is on Step 3 therapy (medium-dose ICS). She continues to have breakthrough symptoms. What is the appropriate Step 4 addition?","a":["Switch to oral corticosteroids daily","Add LABA to ICS (fixed-dose combination) OR increase to high-dose ICS","Add montelukast alone","Switch to ipratropium only"],"correct":1,"rationale":"NAEPP Step 4 therapy for moderate persistent asthma: preferred treatment is medium-to-high-dose ICS + LABA combination. Alternatives include medium-dose ICS + montelukast. Oral steroids are reserved for severe, refractory cases and used at the lowest effective dose for shortest duration. **Board tip:** Step-up therapy if asthma is not controlled for >2-4 weeks; step-down once well-controlled for ≥3 months."},
  {"topic":"Asthma Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"Which immunotherapy option is now FDA-approved as an add-on therapy for severe uncontrolled asthma with eosinophilic phenotype in children ≥6 years?","a":["Oral theophylline","Omalizumab (Xolair) for allergic asthma; mepolizumab (Nucala) for eosinophilic asthma","Systemic daily prednisone","Subcutaneous epinephrine monthly"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Biologic therapies are available for severe asthma: Omalizumab (anti-IgE, age ≥6) for allergic asthma; Mepolizumab (anti-IL-5, age ≥6) for eosinophilic asthma. These are Step 5-6 add-on therapies when high-dose ICS + LABA fails. **Board tip:** Biologics are typically managed by allergist/pulmonologist. Dupilumab (anti-IL-4/IL-13) is another option approved for ≥6 years with moderate-to-severe asthma."},

  # ===== AUTISM SPECTRUM (10) =====
  {"topic":"Autism Spectrum Disorder","course":"D119","q":"The M-CHAT-R/F is a validated screening tool for autism spectrum disorder. At what well-child visits should this be routinely administered?","a":["9 months and 12 months","18 months and 24 months","36 months and 48 months","Only when parents express concern"],"correct":1,"rationale":"AAP recommends universal autism screening using the M-CHAT-R/F (Modified Checklist for Autism in Toddlers, Revised with Follow-Up) at the 18-month AND 24-month well-child visits for all children, regardless of parental concern. **Board tip:** The M-CHAT is a PARENT-COMPLETED questionnaire (23 yes/no items). A positive screen requires follow-up (F) interview and referral — it is a screen, not a diagnosis."},
  {"topic":"Autism Spectrum Disorder","course":"D119","q":"A 2-year-old boy does not respond to his name, makes no eye contact, has no words, does not point to objects, and engages in repetitive hand-flapping. What is the most appropriate next step?","a":["Reassure parents — boys develop more slowly","Immediate referral for comprehensive developmental evaluation AND early intervention services simultaneously","Schedule follow-up at 3-year visit","Refer to neurology for EEG"],"correct":1,"rationale":"These are major autism red flags (no words by 12 months, no pointing/waving by 12 months, no 2-word phrases by 24 months, social communication deficits). Do NOT wait — refer simultaneously for: comprehensive diagnostic evaluation (developmental pediatrics/psychology) AND early intervention services. **Board tip:** 'Don't wait to diagnose before referring to early intervention.' Services can begin while evaluation is pending. Earlier intervention = better outcomes."},
  {"topic":"Autism Spectrum Disorder","course":"D119","q":"DSM-5 diagnosis of Autism Spectrum Disorder requires deficits in which two core domains?","a":["Language and cognitive function","Social communication/interaction AND restricted, repetitive behaviors/interests","Attention and executive function","Sensory processing and motor coordination"],"correct":1,"rationale":"DSM-5 ASD requires persistent deficits in: (1) Social communication and interaction (across multiple contexts) AND (2) Restricted, repetitive patterns of behavior, interests, or activities. Symptoms must be present in early developmental period, cause clinically significant impairment, and not be better explained by another diagnosis. **Board tip:** DSM-5 merged all previous autism diagnoses (Asperger's, PDD-NOS, autistic disorder) under one umbrella term: ASD with severity level (1, 2, or 3 based on support needed)."},
  {"topic":"Autism Spectrum Disorder","course":"D119","q":"Which therapy is considered the gold-standard behavioral intervention for autism spectrum disorder?","a":["Play therapy","Applied Behavior Analysis (ABA)","Cognitive Behavioral Therapy","Occupational therapy alone"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Applied Behavior Analysis (ABA) is the most evidence-based behavioral intervention for ASD, with decades of research supporting improvements in communication, social skills, adaptive behavior, and reducing problem behaviors. Early intensive ABA (25-40 hours/week) in young children shows the strongest outcomes. **Board tip:** ABA is covered by most insurance plans (mandated by law in most states). Speech therapy and OT are important adjunctive therapies but ABA is the primary evidence-based treatment."},
  {"topic":"Autism Spectrum Disorder","course":"D119","q":"A parent asks if vaccines caused their child's autism. What is the evidence-based response?","a":["The MMR vaccine has a small risk of causing autism in genetically susceptible children","There is no scientific evidence linking any vaccine to autism — multiple large studies have definitively disproven this association","Thimerosal in flu shots causes autism","Spreading vaccines out over a longer schedule reduces autism risk"],"correct":1,"rationale":"The purported vaccine-autism link originated from a 1998 Wakefield study that was fraudulent, retracted, and Wakefield lost his medical license. Numerous large-scale studies (millions of children) have definitively found NO association between vaccines (including MMR) and autism. **Board tip:** Thimerosal has been removed from childhood vaccines since 2001 (except some multi-dose flu vials). Autism diagnoses continued to rise after thimerosal removal, further disproving the link."},
  {"topic":"Autism Spectrum Disorder","course":"D119","q":"Which co-occurring condition is present in approximately 30-50% of children with ASD and requires separate screening and treatment?","a":["Celiac disease","Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD)","Type 1 diabetes","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis"],"correct":1,"rationale":"ADHD co-occurs in 30-50% of ASD children, and since DSM-5 now ALLOWS dual diagnosis (previously prohibited), ADHD should be screened for and treated separately. Other common comorbidities: anxiety (40%), intellectual disability (30%), epilepsy (25-30%), depression, and GI problems. **Board tip:** Treating ADHD in ASD can significantly improve functioning. Stimulants work but at lower doses — start low, go slow, as children with ASD are more sensitive to side effects."},
  {"topic":"Autism Spectrum Disorder","course":"D119","q":"A 5-year-old with ASD has severe self-injurious behavior (head-banging) that is not responding to behavioral therapy alone. Which FDA-approved medication may be considered for irritability in ASD?","a":["Methylphenidate (Ritalin)","Aripiprazole (Abilify) or risperidone (Risperdal)","Sertraline (Zoloft)","Atomoxetine (Strattera)"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Aripiprazole and risperidone are the ONLY two FDA-approved medications for irritability associated with ASD (tantrums, aggression, self-injury). They are atypical antipsychotics. **Board tip:** Medications treat SYMPTOMS in ASD, not the core disorder. Behavioral therapy is always first-line. Metabolic monitoring (weight, glucose, lipids) is required with atypical antipsychotic use."},
  {"topic":"Autism Spectrum Disorder","course":"D119","q":"When should autism screening with M-CHAT be additionally administered OUTSIDE of the routine 18 and 24-month schedule?","a":["At every well-child visit from birth","Whenever a parent, teacher, or clinician expresses concern about social or communication development","Only at the 18-month visit","At school-entry physical (age 5)"],"correct":1,"rationale":"While routine screening occurs at 18 and 24 months, additional M-CHAT screening should occur ANYTIME a provider, parent, or teacher expresses concern about a child's social communication, eye contact, language, or behavior — regardless of age or prior screening. **Board tip:** Autism can be reliably diagnosed as early as 18-24 months by experienced clinicians. Older children with milder presentations (especially girls) may not be diagnosed until school age — maintain vigilance."},
  {"topic":"Autism Spectrum Disorder","course":"D119","q":"In a child with ASD, which communication red flag at 12 months warrants immediate developmental evaluation?","a":["Only 5 words spoken","Not yet walking independently","No babbling, no pointing, and no social smiling","Head circumference above 50th percentile"],"correct":2,"rationale":"Absolute language red flags requiring immediate evaluation: No babbling by 12 months, no gestures (pointing, waving) by 12 months, no single words by 16 months, no 2-word phrases by 24 months, or ANY loss of previously acquired language or social skills at ANY age. **Board tip:** Regression (loss of previously acquired skills) at any age is an URGENT red flag for autism and requires immediate referral — do not take a 'wait and see' approach."},
  {"topic":"Autism Spectrum Disorder","course":"D119","q":"A culturally competent FNP is evaluating a Somali-American toddler for suspected ASD. What consideration is most important?","a":["ASD does not occur in non-Western populations","Cultural factors do not affect ASD presentation","Consider cultural and linguistic factors; use culturally adapted screening tools and interpreters; avoid attributing delays to bilingualism alone","Automatically reduce the threshold for diagnosis in minority children"],"correct":2,"rationale":"Cultural and language factors significantly affect ASD screening and diagnosis. Bilingualism does not cause autism or language delay, but cultural norms around eye contact, communication styles, and family structure can affect test performance. Certified medical interpreters are essential — family members should not interpret. **Board tip:** ASD is underdiagnosed in minority, rural, and lower socioeconomic populations due to barriers in access to specialists, cultural stigma, and language barriers. The FNP role in reducing disparities is crucial."},
]

